**Read:** [Phase Transitions — Bhadeshia and Yan](../sources/Phase-Transitions-Bhadeshia-Yan.lingua#r_2PMPJ2B4GPGDT62S567TX1DCPJ), the entire diffusion and further diffusion-mechanism chapters; [Metals, alloys, steels, and graduate modeling — Bhadeshia teaching materials](../sources/Metals-Alloys-and-Graduate-Teaching-Bhadeshia.lingua#r_0HZJKN8WC6SEWA0DABM8P4HC3F), the full atomic diffusion notes, question sheets, and graduate irreversible-process material; [Materials Processing — Chris Schuh, MIT 3.044](../sources/Materials-Processing-MIT-3-044.lingua#r_4V5BA7W1TFKGRTGNHN7ZTAK1TW), all applicable mass-transfer and transient-model exercises.

Define the species, phase, reference frame, mechanism, and driving force. Vacancy/interstitial motion, grain boundaries, interfaces, dislocations, and coupled multicomponent diffusion can produce different transport behavior. A tracer diffusivity, interdiffusion coefficient, and fitted effective coefficient are not automatically identical.

For constant D on a semi-infinite one-dimensional domain initially at concentration c₀ with a fixed surface concentration cₛ for t > 0, c(x,t) = cₛ + (c₀−cₛ) erf[x/(2√Dt)] under the stated model. Verify the initial, surface, and far-field limits. A temperature history generally changes diffusivity and may also change phases, boundaries, and available diffusion paths.

For invented D = 10⁻¹⁴ m² s⁻¹ and t = 3600 s, √Dt = 6 μm; this is a characteristic scale, not a universally defined penetration depth. The concentration at x = 12 μm uses erf(1). Interpret a profile through its boundary/history evidence rather than assigning a single diffusivity from one apparent width.

**Work:** reconstruct a full diffusion/couple or heat-treatment study. Derive the appropriate profile model, independently check mass and limiting cases, compare two possible mechanisms/boundaries, and fit/test parameters on separate evidence. Explain how preparation, spatial resolution, phase change, and temperature uncertainty affect the result. Follow the shared numerical-validation step before the original finite-volume example, which deliberately uses a different, exactly stated periodic domain.

**Continue:** [Build a numerical validation argument across several kinds of evidence](../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/numerics/A-numerical-validation-study.lingua#r_7EY9S6DXHFHSJAGA30W35A4ZD8).

